REFER SHEET 28 FOR TREE LIST.\" This suggests a separate document contains the full schedule of these IDs).*\n\n### 3) Trees within the overhead electric line path\nYes. There is an overhead electric line running roughly parallel to the Southern property boundary (bottom of the page), identified by a line periodically labeled **\"OHE\"**. \n\nScanning this OHE line from West to East:\n*   Near the Southwest corner, the OHE line passes directly through the canopy circle of a large tree (appears to be a removal, but symbol overlap makes it difficult to definitively assign the status).\n*   South of Bldg. 8 (near Unit 806), the OHE line intersects the canopy circle of a tree marked for removal (dashed circle with 'X').\n*   In the Southeast corner cluster (South of Bldg. 6/FH symbol), the OHE line passes through the canopies of a standard tree marked to remain and a large Heritage tree marked to remain. \n\n### 4) Marking for removal versus preservation (Legend Reference)\nThe determination of whether a tree is to be removed or preserved is dictated by the **\"KEY NOTES\"** legend located on the right side of the page. Transcribing exactly as it appears on the document:\n\n**To Remain (Preservation):**\n*   **\"EXISTING HERITAGE TREE - TO REMAIN\"**: Symbolized by a circle with a thick solid outline, a smaller inner circle, and a solid dot in the center. (e.g., Trees 4015, 4040).\n*   **\"EXISTING TREE - TO REMAIN\"**: Symbolized by a circle with a thin solid outline and a solid dot in the center. (e.g., Trees 4058, 4091).\n\n**To Be Removed:**\n*   **\"EXISTING TREE - TO BE REMOVED\"**: Symbolized by a circle with a dashed outline and a large 'X' drawn through the center of the circle. (e.g., Tree 4093).\n\nAdditionally, the legend notes:\n*   **\"TREE PROTECTION FENCING - REF DETAIL 1/SHEET 6\"**: Symbolized by a distinct line consisting of a dash followed by two dots, forming enclosed shapes around groups of preserved trees.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSi