{"level":30,"time":1776445578292,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RNhParhGAQzHLCzTVX1NWC","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 11 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n     4→\n     5→[graphic scale bar with increments 0, 20, 40]\n     6→Scale 1\" = 20'\n     7→\n     8→## Block 2: drawing — This is a detailed grading plan for a multi-family development. It shows existing and proposed contours, building footprints, retaining walls, storm drainage infrastructure, and various utility easements. Key features include multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each with finished floor elevations (FFE). The plan also indicates existing structures, vegetation, and adjacent property information.\n     9→\n    10→The plan displays a comprehensive layout for a multi-family development. It includes:\n    11→\n    12→*   **Building Footprints:** Eight buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, with their respective square footages and finished floor elevations (FFE). For example, Bldg. 1 is 3,290 sf with an FFE of 687.50, and Bldg. 8 is 4,997 sf with an FFE of 685.50.\n    13→*   **Topography:** Existing and proposed contour lines indicating the site's elevation changes.\n    14→*   **Infrastructure:** Proposed storm drain lines, area inlets, and concrete retaining walls.\n    15→*   **Easements:** Various easements are depicted, including drainage easements, wastewater easements, electric and telephone easements, and a greenbelt access and use easement.\n    16→*   **Adjacent Properties:** Information on adjacent properties, such as \"Lot A Cortex Heights Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-Family\" and \"William Lance Corsbie, Jr. 1.967 Acres Zoning: SF-3 Use: Single Family\".\n    17→*   **Existing Features:** Existing trees, rock rubble, and stone retaining walls.\n    18→*   **Utilities:** Locations of existing water manholes, gas meters, wastewater manholes, and AT&T vaults.\n    19→*   **Specific Details:** The plan includes specific details like a sediment basin, an existing retaining wall to be replaced, and a sidewalk easement.\n    20→\n    21→## Block 3: notes — A note providing specific instructions regarding the construction of grading lines related to curbs.\n    22→\n    23→Curb Note:\n    24→Grading lines that indicate flows toward the curb shall be constructed as a catch curb. Grade lines that indicate flows\n    25→away from curb shall be constructed as a spill curb.\n    26→\n    27→## Block 4: notes — This is the legend for the grading plan, explaining the symbols and line types used to represent various features on the drawing, such as contours, spot elevations, curbs, limits of construction, storm drains, level landings, and ADA ramps.\n    28→\n    29→Legend\n    30→[dashed line] --700-- Existing Contour\n    31→[solid line] --700-- Proposed Contour\n    32→[plus sign] 698.25 Proposed Spot Elevation\n    33→700.00\n    34→699.50 Top of Curb\n    35→Bottom of Curb\n    36→[solid line with LOC] Proposed Limits of Construction\n    37→[dashed line with SD] Proposed Storm Drain Line\n    38→L= Level Landing\n    39→(Not to exceed 2% in any Direction)\n    40→Down [arrow] ADA Ramp\n    41→(Not to exceed 8.33%)\n    42→\n    43→## Block 5: notes — Provides the legal description of the property being developed.\n    44→\n    45→Legal Description\n    46→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    47→\n    48→## Block 6: notes — Details two benchmark points used for elevation reference on the site plan.\n    49→\n    50→Benchmarks\n    51→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    52→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    53→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    54→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    55→\n    56→## Block 7: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for a list of trees.\n    57→\n    58→Tree List\n    59→Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n    60→\n    61→## Block 11: text_block — A clear instruction to call before starting any excavation work.\n    62→\n    63→Call Before You Dig!!\n    64→\n    65→## Block 12: text_block — A general disclaimer stating that the application does not constitute verification of all data, and the engineer of record is responsible for its completeness and accuracy.\n    66→\n    67→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\n    68→applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\n    69→submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    70→\n    71→## Block 13: text_block — A note advising that existing utility locations are based on record information and must be field-verified by the contractor prior to construction.\n    72→\n    73→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not\n    74→match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility\n    75→locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning\n    76→construction.\n    77→\n    78→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60b9cdbc-bfc6-4085-b89d-09c184480485","uuid":"8207bc0f-cfe1-45f6-a405-26edaaf99991","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-11/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 11 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n\n[graphic scale bar with increments 0, 20, 40]\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This is a detailed grading plan for a multi-family development. It shows existing and proposed contours, building footprints, retaining walls, storm drainage infrastructure, and various utility easements. Key features include multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each with finished floor elevations (FFE). The plan also indicates existing structures, vegetation, and adjacent property information.\n\nThe plan displays a comprehensive layout for a multi-family development. It includes:\n\n*   **Building Footprints:** Eight buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, with their respective square footages and finished floor elevations (FFE). For example, Bldg. 1 is 3,290 sf with an FFE of 687.50, and Bldg. 8 is 4,997 sf with an FFE of 685.50.\n*   **Topography:** Existing and proposed contour lines indicating the site's elevation changes.\n*   **Infrastructure:** Proposed storm drain lines, area inlets, and concrete retaining walls.\n*   **Easements:** Various easements are depicted, including drainage easements, wastewater easements, electric and telephone easements, and a greenbelt access and use easement.\n*   **Adjacent Properties:** Information on adjacent properties, such as \"Lot A Cortex Heights Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-Family\" and \"William Lance Corsbie, Jr. 1.967 Acres Zoning: SF-3 Use: Single Family\".\n*   **Existing Features:** Existing trees, rock rubble, and stone retaining walls.\n*   **Utilities:** Locations of existing water manholes, gas meters, wastewater manholes, and AT&T vaults.\n*   **Specific Details:** The plan includes specific details like a sediment basin, an existing retaining wall to be replaced, and a sidewalk easement.\n\n## Block 3: notes — A note providing specific instructions regarding the construction of grading lines related to curbs.\n\nCurb Note:\nGrading lines that indicate flows toward the curb shall be constructed as a catch curb. Grade lines that indicate flows\naway from curb shall be constructed as a spill curb.\n\n## Block 4: notes — This is the legend for the grading plan, explaining the symbols and line types used to represent various features on the drawing, such as contours, spot elevations, curbs, limits of construction, storm drains, level landings, and ADA ramps.\n\nLegend\n[dashed line] --700-- Existing Contour\n[solid line] --700-- Proposed Contour\n[plus sign] 698.25 Proposed Spot Elevation\n700.00\n699.50 Top of Curb\nBottom of Curb\n[solid line with LOC] Proposed Limits of Construction\n[dashed line with SD] Proposed Storm Drain Line\nL= Level Landing\n(Not to exceed 2% in any Direction)\nDown [arrow] ADA Ramp\n(Not to exceed 8.33%)\n\n## Block 5: notes — Provides the legal description of the property being developed.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 6: notes — Details two benchmark points used for elevation reference on the site plan.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 7: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for a list of trees.\n\nTree List\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n## Block 11: text_block — A clear instruction to call before starting any excavation work.\n\nCall Before You Dig!!\n\n## Block 12: text_block — A general disclaimer stating that the application does not constitute verification of all data, and the engineer of record is responsible for its completeness and accuracy.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\napplicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\nsubmittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\n## Block 13: text_block — A note advising that existing utility locations are based on record information and must be field-verified by the contractor prior to construction.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not\nmatch locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility\nlocations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning\nconstruction.\n\n","numLines":78,"startLine":1,"totalLines":78}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776445578292,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01JxQLPv4eCFV2a5e1rxLJdw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! The grading plan shows \"various utility easements\" including:\n- Drainage easements\n- Wastewater easements  \n- Electric and telephone easements\n- Greenbelt access and use easement\n\nBut the blocks don't specify if there are transmission easements. From the facts.md, I know there are 5' electric easements that are plat-dedicated, but no transmission easement was mentioned.\n\nNow I have enough information to make my findings. Let me compile all the checklist items and their determinations:\n\n## CHECKLIST EVALUATION:\n\n**EL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor**\n- Applicability: YES (OHE line is shown on landscape plan)\n- Evidence:\n  - OHE line shown on southern boundary of landscape plan (Sheet 31)\n  - Three trees identified in proximity to the OHE line (vision analysis)\n  - No dimension lines showing clearances\n  - Plant schedule identifies species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel) but DOES NOT indicate utility compatibility (UC designation from ECM Appendix F)\n  - Cannot determine from available plans which trees are utility-compatible and which are not\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n  - Cannot verify clearances due to lack of dimension lines\n  - Cannot verify utility compatibility designation without access to ECM Appendix F reference on the plans\n\n**EL-2.2: Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor**\n- Applicability: NO\n- Evidence: No transmission lines (≥60kV) or transmission easements identified on the site plans or facts.md\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.3: Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor**\n\n\n- Applicability: YES (OHE line present with potential large trees nearby)\n- Evidence:\n  - OHE line shown on southern boundary\n  - Cedar Elm and Live Oak species listed in plant schedule typically exceed 40 feet at maturity\n  - Plan only indicates \"16' HT minimum\" which represents initial height, not mature height\n  - Mature heights are not specified on the plans\n  - No dimension lines showing clearances from conductor\n  - Cannot verify which trees fall within 25-foot setback\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n  - Mature heights not specified on plans (see EL-2.11)\n  - Clearances not dimensioned\n\n**EL-2.4: Large trees (mature height 40+\n\n**\n\nfeet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor**\n- Applicability: NO (no transmission lines)\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.5: Trees (any species) proposed within transmission easement boundaries**\n- Applicability: NO\n- Evidence: No transmission easements identified on the site\n- Status: N/A\n\n**EL-2.6: Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility pole**\n- Applicability: CONDITIONAL—only if poles are shown\n- Evidence:\n  - OHE utility line depicted on landscape plan (Sheet 31) but utility poles not explicitly marked\n  - Vision analysis did not identify utility poles\n  - Cannot verify pole locations relative to trees\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n  - Utility pole locations not clearly shown on landscape plan in relation to proposed trees\n\n**EL-2.7: Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted electric equipment**\n- Applicability: YES (pad-mounted transform\n\ners shown on electrical design plan)\n- Evidence: Electrical Design Plan (Sheet 21) identifies transformer pads, but landscape plan (Sheet 31) lacks transformer locations, preventing cross-reference with tree placements. Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE—pad-mounted equipment locations absent from landscape plan.\n\n**EL-2.8: Trees proposed within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment**\n- Applicability: YES (underground equipment present on electrical plan)\n- Evidence: Underground pull boxes, vaults, and buried transformers shown on Electrical Design Plan (Sheet 21), but not depicted on landscape plan (Sheet 31). Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE—cannot cross-reference underground equipment with tree locations.\n\n**EL-2.9: Trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment lack required root barriers**\n- Applicability: YES (contingent on EL-2.8)\n- Evidence: Equipment locations not shown on landscape plan; root barrier specifications not visible in landscape details. Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE—cannot confirm tree proximity to underground equipment or verify root barrier presence.\n\n**EL-2.10: Plant legend or plant schedule does not identify species of trees proposed near electric facilities**\n- Applicability: YES (trees located near electric facilities)\n- Evidence: Plant schedule (Sheet 35) identifies all species by common and botanical names—Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia), Live Oak (Quercus virginiana), Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana), Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora). Landscape Plan (Sheet 31) shows tree locations relative to overhead electric line with legend defining tree symbols. Status: PASS—species are identified and trees are shown in relation to overhead electric facilities.\n\n**EL-2.11: Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) not differentiated on plans**\n- Applicability: YES (large trees are proposed)\n- Evidence: Cedar Elm and Live Oak typically mature to 40+ feet. Plant schedule lists only container-grown heights (\"16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN\") without mature heights. Landscape plan fails to differentiate between large trees (40+ feet at maturity) and smaller species. No plan notes address mature tree heights or differentiation.\n- Status: FAIL\n\n**EL-2.12: Tree clearances measured from utility pole centerline instead of conductor**\n- Applicability: NO\n- Evidence: No clearance dimensions appear on the plans\n- Status: PASS\n\n**EL-2.13: Tree clearances measured from tree canopy instead of trunk**\n- Applicability: NO\n- Evidence: No clearance dimensions appear on the plans\n- Status: PASS\n\n**EL\n\n-2.14: Trees not shown on landscape plan in relation to overhead electric facilities**\n- Applicability: YES\n- Evidence: Both the OHE line and trees are depicted on Sheet 31, and the vision analysis confirms trees exist near the OHE line\n- Status: FAIL\n  - While trees and the OHE line are both visible on the plan, no distance dimensions are provided between them, which prevents verification of adequate clearances\n\n**EL-2.15: Plans propose trees within prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy written approval**\n- Applicability: CONDITIONAL—depends on findings from items 1-9\n- Evidence: Three trees appear in close proximity to the OHE line at the SW and SE corners per the vision analysis, but dimension lines showing clearances are absent, and no Austin Energy approval documentation is visible\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n  - Cannot confirm whether trees fall within the 15-foot prohibited zone for distribution lines\n  - No Austin Energy written approval letter is evident in the submitted plans\n\n**EL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment**\n\nThe core question is whether non-utility-compatible trees are positioned within 15 feet of the OHE line. The landscape plan shows the OHE line and three trees in proximity to it at the SW and SE corners, but without dimension lines indicating the actual distances, I cannot definitively establish whether they fall within the 15-foot threshold.\n\nLooking at the plant schedule, the species are identified but lack UC (utility-compatible) designations. According to ECM Appendix F, utility-compatible status is marked with an \"x\" in the UC column—this designation is the definitive standard, not tree height alone. From the grouping examples, Texas Mountain Laurel qualifies as utility-compatible, while Cedar Elm and Live Oak have uncertain compatibility status. Without the plant schedule explicitly referencing Appendix F or providing UC designations for each species, I cannot confirm which trees are non-utility-compatible or verify their distances from the OHE line.\n\nStatus: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n. The plans don't document utility compatibility against Appendix F, and without dimensioned clearances, I cannot determine if non-utility-compatible species are specifically within the 15-foot lateral zone. The spatial relationships and species identifications near the overhead line remain unconfirmed.\n\nGiven the missing clearance dimensions and undocumented utility compatibility, I cannot verify compliance with EL-2.1. The absence of these required elements—dimensional callouts showing distances and documented UC status for each species—constitutes a deficiency in the submission itself. The landscape plan shows the overhead electric line location but lacks dimension lines indicating clearances from trees to the conductor. This mirrors the incomplete documentation issue—the required clearance information simply isn't provided on the plans. The key question becomes whether this is a plan deficiency (FAIL) or an inability to verify compliance (NOT-VERIFIABLE). Since the plans don't show trees explicitly positioned within prohibited zones, but rather fail to document the clearance distances needed to make that determination, this appears to be a NOT-VERIFIABLE situation rather than a documented violation.\n\nFor the checklist items, the distinction matters: EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, EL-2.9, and EL-2.15 all hinge on clearance distances that cannot be verified from the plans, so they're NOT-VERIFIABLE. EL-2.11 shows a clear deficiency. EL-2.10 warrants another look—the plant schedule does identify species by both common and botanical names for each proposed tree, which satisfies the requirement, making it a PASS. The remaining items (EL-2.2, EL-2.4, EL-2.5) don't apply to this situation.\n\nFor EL-2.10 specifically, I need to determine whether the checklist is asking if species are identified anywhere in the documentation, or if they're identified on the landscape plan itself near the electric facilities. The landscape plan uses symbols (SY, PL, P, M) that correlate to the plant schedule, so identifying the species requires cross-referencing. The question is whether this indirect identification method adequately addresses the requirement that species be identified for trees proposed near electric facilities.\n\nThe plant schedule clearly lists the species corresponding to each symbol, so the information exists and is accessible. This satisfies the requirement—it's a PASS.\n\nFor EL-2.11, the issue is different. The requirement asks whether large trees (40+ feet mature height) are differentiated on the plans to verify clearances from overhead electric lines. The landscape plan proposes Cedar Elm and Live Oak, both of which typically exceed 40 feet at maturity, but the plans don't indicate mature heights and don't mark or distinguish large trees from smaller ones. Without this differentiation, there's no way to verify adequate clearance from the electric facilities. This is a clear deficiency—a FAIL.\n\nOn EL-2.6, the applicability hinges on whether overhead electric poles are present or proposed. The landscape plan shows the overhead electric line but doesn't explicitly depict utility poles. The electrical design plan shows transformers and underground infrastructure but not poles themselves. Since poles aren't clearly shown on the plans, I can't measure distances from proposed trees to poles. This appears to fall outside the applicability trigger, making it potentially N/A rather than NOT-VERIFIABLE, though the presence of an overhead line typically implies poles exist somewhere along that line.\n\nI'll take the conservative position and mark EL-2.6 as NOT-VERIFIABLE since pole locations aren't documented on the landscape plan, preventing clearance verification.\n\nCompiling the final assessment: EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, and EL-2.9 are all NOT-VERIFIABLE due to missing utility infrastructure locations or undocumented clearances. EL-2.11 fails because large trees lack differentiation and mature height specifications. EL-2.15 is NOT-VERIFIABLE since I cannot confirm trees are outside prohibited zones and no Austin Energy approval is documented.\n\nNow reconsidering the applicability of several items. EL-2.6 is uncertain—while the OHE line exists, poles aren't explicitly shown. However, EL-2.7 and EL-2.8 are clearly applicable because the Electrical Design Plan documents pad-mounted transformers and underground equipment like pull boxes and vaults. The real issue is that these equipment locations don't appear on the landscape plan alongside the proposed trees, creating a coordination gap between sheets rather than a missing element entirely.\n\nThis makes the clearance verification impossible to confirm from the submitted documents. Without overlaying both plans or cross-referencing them, I can't determine if adequate separation exists. The information needed to verify compliance simply isn't presented in a way that allows assessment.\n\nMy conclusions: EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, EL-2.9, and EL-2.15 are NOT-VERIFIABLE due to insufficient coordination between plans, while EL-2.11 is a FAIL.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1841,"cache_read_input_tokens":70187,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1841},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60b9cdbc-bfc6-4085-b89d-09c184480485","uuid":"db93721a-7cb2-4b28-a9d8-5cfbaa6da35e"}
