osed Mitigation: 0.50 |\n| 4012 | 13 | Unknown | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 0.50 |\n| 4015 | 24 | Pecan | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n| 4016 | 15 | China Berry | Remain; Invasive Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 0.00 |\n| 4017 | 10 | China Berry | Remain; Invasive Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 0.00 |\n| 4018 | 32 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n| 4019 | 42 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n| 4020 | 10 | Hackberry | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 0.50 |\n| 4026 | 36 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n| 4027 | 27 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n| 4028 | 20 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 1.00 |\n| 4030a | 15 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 1.00 |\n| 4030b | 18 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 1.00 |\n| 4038 | 24 | Live Oak | Remain; Good Condition; Proposed Mitigation: 3.00 |\n\n***\n\n### Analysis of Utility-Compatible Species\n\nYou asked if any of the trees are utility-compatible species (typically small trees under 20 feet tall). \n\nLooking at the species list of the trees being **retained** (Live Oak, China Berry, Cedar Elm, Hackberry, Pecan), **none of these are typically considered utility-compatible.** \n*   Live Oak, Cedar Elm, Hackberry, and Pecan are all large canopy shade trees that grow significantly taller than 20 feet and would interfere with overhead lines.\n*   China Berry is an invasive species (as noted in the table) and also generally grows larger than 20 feet. \n\n*(Note: If you are looking for utility-compatible species proposed for *new* plantings, you will need to review the landscape plan or the actual proposed mitigation planting schedule, which is not present on this specific sheet).*"}]}
{"level":30,"time":1776445578298,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G2mPzmeFJjCzVJQevzf241","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape site plan, here are the details regarding the southern boundary and the overhead electric (OHE) line:\n\n**1) Tree Canopies near the OHE Line (within approx. 20-25 feet):**\nLocating the \"----OHE----\" line running horizontally along the bottom (southern) edge of the drawing, there is only one cluster of trees in close proximity to it. This cluster is located at the far eastern end of the southern boundary (the southeast corner of the site).\nThere are exactly **three (3)** prominent tree canopy circles in this cluster. Based on the visual scale provided at the bottom right (`SCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")`), the centers of all three trees appear to be well within 20-25 feet of the OHE line (visually appearing closer to 10-15 feet away).\n\n**2) Symbols and Markings on these Trees:**\nYes, these three trees are marked with specific symbols at their center points. Looking at the cluster from west to east:\n*   **Tree 1 (Westernmost of the three):** The center point is marked with a cross (**+**).\n*   **Tree 2 (Middle):** The center point is marked with an \"**x**\". It also has the letter \"**M**\" positioned directly next to the center mark. Referencing the text block beneath the \"KEY NOTES\" section on the right, \"M\" is defined as exactly: \"**M - MITIGATION TREE**\".\n*   **Tree 3 (Easternmost):** The center point is marked with a cross (**+**). The tree canopy is drawn with a dashed circular line rather than a solid one.\n\n**3) Spacing/Distance Between Individual Trees near the OHE:**\nUsing the provided `1\"=20'-0\"` scale bar for visual estimation, these three trees are planted very closely together. The center-to-center distance between the first tree and the second tree, as well as between the second tree and the third tree, appears to be approximately **10 to 15 feet**.\n\n**4) Building Structures or Parking Areas Between Trees and OHE Line:**\n**No**, there are no building structures or parking areas located between these three trees and the \"----OHE----\" line. The plan shows these trees situated in an open landscape or buffer strip at the edge of the property. The building footprint and parking areas are located further north (above) these trees, separated by a storm drain line (\"----SD----\") and a Limits of Construction line (\"----LOC----\").\n\n**5) Layout of Trees from West to East Along the Southern Boundary:**\nTracing the path parallel to the \"----OHE----\" line from the western edge of the site to the eastern edge:\n*   **Western portion:** There are no tree canopies shown along the boundary.\n*   **Central portion:** There are no tree canopies shown. The space contains only property lines, the \"----SD----\" line, and adjacent building structures located further north.\n*   **Eastern portion:** The southern boundary remains completely clear of trees until the very far east end (southeast corner), where the layout abruptly features the tight cluster of three closely spaced trees detailed in point 1."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"848e4c7a-0ebd-4c8c-8f6e-1da50c4b3f33","uuid":"40d791b1-10ee-4e5d-b02d-63aa4e240ea4","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape site plan, here are the details regarding the southern boundary and the overhead electric (OHE) line:\n\n**1) Tree Canopies near the OHE Line (within approx. 20-25 feet):**\nLocating the \"----OHE----\" line running horizontally along the bottom (southern) edge of the drawing, there is only one cluster of trees in close proximity to it. This cluster is located at the far eastern end of the southern boundary (the southeast corner of the site).\nThe