 degrees, and secondary-to-primary crossing details are incomplete.\n\nNo marquee signs are proposed, so that's not applicable. Pole relocation or new poles aren't part of this work. Decorative lighting may be present and should be checked for conflicts. Trees within clearance zones fail compatibility standards. Meter locations need obstruction verification. There are no streetlights on the property. Down guy conflicts and anchor easements require checking if such guys exist on site. Transformers aren't shown on the plans.\n\nNiche-mounted transformers aren't indicated. Construction staging appears on the site plan but clearance dimensions can't be verified from available documentation. The 48-hour notification requirement is missing from the plans. Service masts may be shown on the utility plan and need verification.\n\nSeveral items don't apply since the proposed project doesn't include these elements: no substations, transmission lines, vehicle barriers, emergency access structures, vehicle display areas, highway frontage, streetlight poles beneath overhead lines, trash enclosures under OHE, new overhead routing, marquee signage, or comparable infrastructure that would trigger these specific requirements.\n\nFor elements that are present on the site, clearance verification becomes necessary. Building setbacks from overhead lines can't be confirmed without surveyed elevations. Facility clearances and radial distances lack supporting dimensions. Fences appear on the plans and require clearance validation. Elevated structures near drainage or pond areas need assessment.\n\nFence posts attached to poles are depicted but lack detailed measurements for verification. Retaining walls are visible on site and need clearance checks, though section views aren't provided. Pole conflicts with the driveway access should be evaluated. Sidewalk clearances from poles are shown but need confirmation. Distribution line routing over or under facilities requires detailed examination. Water meters and lines appear on utility documentation but can't be fully verified without additional detail. Foundation locations relative to property lines are indicated and need validation. Decorative lighting may be present and should be identified. Meter obstruction clearances need to be assessed based on the plans provided.\n\nDown guy conflicts and anchor easements require checking against the site layout. Transformer pads are marked but clearance distances must be confirmed. The staging area is indicated though specific zones can't be verified from current documentation. Service mast roof clearance should be reviewed on the utility plan.\n\nFrom the site plan analysis, overhead lines don't appear to cross directly over the proposed residential buildings, but the western overhead line does cross over both new driveway entrances. The southern overhead line runs immediately adjacent to the site features.\n\nThe critical items to verify are the driveway entrances under the western overhead line, the retaining wall positioned near the southern overhead line, and trees located under the southern overhead line. I need to examine the wall plan and wall details sheets to find any clearance dimensions specified for the retaining walls.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2390,"cache_read_input_tokens":66053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2390},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f309802-c1eb-4364-b84a-07fe4c2517f2","uuid":"eb7642df-9b05-4ef0-b6bd-cd855e3911c1"}
