{"level":30,"time":1776446286203,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":35,"prompt":"Look at the plant schedules and shade tree list. Identify all the tree species being proposed for mitigation and street yard plantings, including their quantities and sizes. Which of these species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel, etc.) are typical utility-compatible trees that grow to modest heights (under 20 feet)? Are there any notes about planting restrictions or locations relative to utilities?","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on the provided landscape plan document, here is the detailed extraction regarding tree species, utility compatibility, and related notes:\n\n### Proposed Tree Species (From the \"PLANT LIST\")\nThe \"PLANT LIST\" is divided into Shade Trees and Ornamental Trees. Here are the proposed species, quantities, and minimum specifications:\n\n**SHADE TREES**\n*   **Live Oak** (*Quercus virginiana*)\n    *   **Quantity:** 2\n    *   **Size/Min. Spec.:** 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM, CONTAINER GROWN\n*   **Cedar Elm** (*Ulmus crassifolia*)\n    *   **Quantity:** 10\n    *   **Size/Min. Spec.:** 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM, CONTAINER GROWN\n\n**ORNAMENTAL TREES**\n*   **Texas Mountain Laurel** (*Sophora secundiflora*)\n    *   **Quantity:** 2\n    *   **Size/Min. Spec.:** 45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 8' HT. MINIMUM, CONTAINER GROWN\n*   **Mexican Redbud** (*Cercis canadensis var. mexicana*)\n    *   **Quantity:** 8\n    *   **Size/Min. Spec.:** 45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 10' HT. MINIMUM, CONTAINER GROWN\n\n*(Note: The \"COA REQUIRED TREES CHART\" details that a total of 13 tree inches are required for Street yard, Buffer, and Parking Lot, and the proposed plant list provides 68 total inches, leaving 55 inches that may be counted for mitigation.)*\n\n### Utility-Compatible Trees\nUtility-compatible trees typically refer to smaller ornamental species that will not grow into overhead power lines (usually remaining under 20-25 feet at maturity). Based on standard horticultural characteristics of the species listed:\n*   **Utility-Compatible:** The **Texas Mountain Laurel** and **Mexican Redbud** are both considered modest-height ornamental trees/large shrubs that typically stay under 20 feet. These are standard utility-compatible choices.\n*   **Not Utility-Compatible:** The **Live Oak** and **Cedar Elm** are both large canopy shade trees that mature to significant heights (40-60+ feet) and would conflict with overhead utility lines.\n\n### Notes on Planting Restrictions Relative to Utilities\nA thorough review of the textual notes provided on this sheet (\"REMEDIAL TREE CARE NOTES\", \"SPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\", \"SITE DEVELOPMENT PERMIT IRRIGATION NOTES\", and \"SITE DEVELOPMENT PERMIT LANDSCAPE\") reveals **no specific notes regarding planting restrictions or locational setbacks relative to overhead or underground utilities.** \n\nThere are notes concerning trenching restrictions to protect *existing* tree roots (e.g., Note 6 under SITE DEVELOPMENT PERMIT LANDSCAPE: \"TRENCHING SHALL NOT OCCUR WITHIN THE FENCED DRIP LINE AREAS OF EXISTING TREES\"), but nothing governing the placement of newly proposed trees near utility corridors. If utility planting restrictions exist for this project, they are likely detailed on a different sheet (such as a utility plan or standard city detail sheet).","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1393,"outputTokens":674,"totalTokens":3766,"reasoningTokens":1699},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 2584864]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisua