{"level":30,"time":1776446354694,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01VmcFiT8CjQ82CGzhqd1opZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I have the critical mapping:\n\n**From Sheet 35 (Plant Schedule):**\n\nShade Trees symbols:\n- Circle with cross (+) = LIVE OAK (Quercus virginiana)\n- Circle with dot (•) = Should be Cedar Elm per the legend, but I need to verify\n\nOrnamental Trees:\n- TEXAS MOUNTAIN LAUREL (Sophora secundiflora) - Qty 2\n- MEXICAN REDBUD (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana) - Qty 6\n\nWait, there's a discrepancy. The vision output says:\n- Circle with cross (+) = LIVE OAK\n- Circle with dot (•) = (empty circle? or Cedar Elm?)\n\nLet me re-read: \"A circle with a cross (+) represents: LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA\" and \"A circle with a dot (small inner circle) represents: CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA\"\n\nActually, I think there's confusion. The shade trees table from the blocks.md showed:\n```\n| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n```\n\nSo:\n- Circle with dot = EXISTING TREE\n- Circle with cross = LIVE OAK (2 trees)\n- Circle with hash = CEDAR ELM (10 trees)\n\nBut the vision analysis of Sheet 31 said the three trees near the OHE are:\n\n\n1. Tree 1 (westernmost) - marked with cross (+)\n2. Tree 2 (middle) - marked with \"x\" and labeled \"M\" for mitigation\n3. Tree 3 (easternmost) - marked with cross (+)\n\nThe \"x\" and \"+\" symbols might be distinct or the same—I need to clarify whether these represent different tree types or if the marking convention varies across the sheets.\n\nLooking at the shade trees legend, the symbols are: circle with dot for existing trees, circle with cross for Live Oak, and circle with hash for Cedar Elm. The \"x\" marking on Tree 2 could correspond to the hash symbol for Cedar Elm, while the \"+\" symbols on Trees 1 and 3 indicate Live Oak. This would align with the mitigation designation on Tree 2—Cedar Elm is likely the mitigation species being proposed. I need to check the mature size specifications for these species to complete the analysis.\n\nSheet 35 only provides minimum installation heights (16 feet for both species), but the grouping criteria requires mature heights of 40 feet or greater for large trees. Live Oak typically reaches 40-80 feet at maturity, and Cedar Elm grows to 40-60 feet, so both qualify as large trees under the electrical utility threshold.\n\nI need to verify whether these species have utility-compatible (UC) designations, as this would affect compliance. The plan shows three trees—two Live Oaks and one Cedar Elm—positioned 10-15 feet from the overhead electric line without documented clearances. If these aren't UC-rated, they'd violate EL-2.1 for non-UC trees within 15 feet of distribution conductors, and potentially EL-2.3 regarding large tree placement restrictions.\n\nSeveral other violations emerge: trees appear within 10 feet of utility poles (EL-2.6), large trees aren't differentiated on the landscape plan itself despite being identified in the schedule (EL-2.11), and the relationship between tree locations and overhead facilities may not be sufficiently clear to verify all required clearances (EL-2.14). The plant legend identifies species in the schedule but not directly on the plan, which creates verification challenges. Additionally, I should check whether any trees fall within prohibited zones relative to Austin Energy requirements (EL-2.15).\n\nI also need to verify distances for pad-mounted and underground equipment, confirm whether root barriers are shown for trees near underground utilities, and check if any clearances are dimensioned on the plans. Transmission line-related standards likely don't apply here since no transmission lines appear to be present.\n\nI'll measure the exact distances between the three trees and the overhead electrical line and poles to verify compliance with spacing requirements.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2043,"cache_read_input_tokens":53091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2043},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"848e4c7a-0ebd-4c8c-8f6e-1da50c4b3f33","uuid":"3aba4924-066f-4cb7-a433-d2fea78c8adc"}
