{"level":30,"time":1776446612764,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuVG8LjsFLzkSHzmxB8FRM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 38 — Wall Plan\n     2→\n     3→Plan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet provides the structural details, plan view, and specifications for retaining walls, including a 14'-0\" maximum height retaining wall section. Its primary purpose is to guide the construction, reinforcement, and inspection of the site's retaining walls, footings, and associated slab tie-ins.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values**:\n    10→* Maximum retaining wall height: 14'-0\"\n    11→* Concrete compressive strength: 3,000 PSI (minimum 28-day)\n    12→* Allowable soil bearing: 4,000 PSF\n    13→* Active soil pressure (heel): 45.5 PSF\n    14→* Passive pressure: 290 PSF\n    15→* Soil weight: 130 PSF\n    16→* Reinforcing steel grades: Grade 60 (for #4 rebar and larger), Grade 40 (for #3 rebar)\n    17→* Wall vertical reinforcement: #7 @ 10\" O.C.\n    18→* Wall horizontal reinforcement: #3 @ 12\" O.C. (each face)\n    19→* Minimum concrete clear cover: 3\" for surfaces in contact with earth; 1 1/2\" for walls/footings adjacent to exterior/interior surfaces; 1 1/2\" above sub-grade for slabs\n    20→* Dowel embedment: 6\" embedment for #6 rebar dowels into existing concrete\n    21→\n    22→**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n    23→\n    24→* **Block 2**: diagram — 14'-0\" Max Retaining Wall cross-section showing rebar spacing, gravel backfill, weep pipes, and geotextile placement. READ for exact rebar sizing and material specifications, VISION for structural layout and dimensions.\n    25→* **Block 4**: drawing — Plan View showing the layout of the new and existing retaining walls, pond walls, and specific doweling instructions for an existing buttress. Use VISION to understand the spatial relationship, alignment, and construction phasing between new and existing walls.\n    26→* **Block 1**: diagram — Dowel Detail showing a cross-section for tying a new slab to an existing slab with #6 rebar. READ for drill bit sizing (7/8\" for #4), epoxy installation instructions, and hand-installation notes.\n    27→* **Block 3**: diagram — Typical Step in Footing elevation view showing standard 2'-0\" horizontal and 12\" vertical step dimensions. Use VISION for step geometry and reinforcement continuity.\n    28→* **Block 10**: table — Design Criteria outlining IBC 2015 soil bearing and lateral pressure requirements. READ for the exact engineering design values.\n    29→* **Block 6**: notes — General and structural notes detailing concrete specifications, clear cover minimums, and reinforcement rules. READ for material grades, cover depths, and corner bar requirements (24\"x24\").\n    30→* **Block 9**: table — Required Inspections list. READ to confirm the three key inspection milestones (footings, walls, backfill/drainage).\n    31→* **Blocks 5, 7, 8**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains a general schematic disclaimer (Block 5), title block data for project 3759 Valley View (Block 7), and engineer seals for MLAW Engineers (Block 8).\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    36→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97c9d1ef-3ef8-4a72-b129-cbdede465e25","uuid":"1e1dd949-b56a-46aa-a290-95b64ae403f1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-38/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 38 — Wall Plan\n\nPlan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides the structural details, plan view, and specifications for retaining walls, including a 14'-0\" maximum height retaining wall section. Its primary purpose is to guide the construction, reinforcement, and inspection of the site's retaining walls, footings, and associated slab tie-ins.\n\n**Key values**:\n* Maximum retaining wall height: 14'-0\"\n* Concrete compressive strength: 3,000 PSI (minimum 28-day)\n* Allowable soil bearing: 4,000 PSF\n* Active soil pressure (heel): 45.5 PSF\n* Passive pressure: 290 PSF\n* Soil weight: 130 PSF\n* Reinforcing steel grades: Grade 60 (for #4 rebar and larger), Grade 40 (for #3 rebar)\n* Wall vertical reinforcement: #7 @ 10\" O.C.\n* Wall horizontal reinforcement: #3 @ 12\" O.C. (each face)\n* Minimum concrete clear cover: 3\" for surfaces in contact with earth; 1 1/2\" for walls/footings adjacent to exterior/interior surfaces; 1 1/2\" above sub-grade for slabs\n* Dowel embedment: 6\" embedment for #6 rebar dowels into existing concrete\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n\n* **Block 2**: diagram — 14'-0\" Max Retaining Wall cross-section showing rebar spacing, gravel backfill, weep pipes, and geotextile placement. READ for exact rebar sizing and material specifications, VISION for structural layout and dimensions.\n* **Block 4**: drawing — Plan View showing the layout of the new and existing retaining walls, pond walls, and specific doweling instructions for an existing buttress. Use VISION to understand the spatial relationship, alignment, and construction phasing between new and existing walls.\n* **Block 1**: diagram — Dowel Detail showing a cross-section for tying a new slab to an existing slab with #6 rebar. READ for drill bit sizing (7/8\" for #4), epoxy installation instructions, and hand-installation notes.\n* **Block 3**: diagram — Typical Step in Footing elevation view showing standard 2'-0\" horizontal and 12\" vertical step dimensions. Use VISION for step geometry and reinforcement continuity.\n* **Block 10**: table — Design Criteria outlining IBC 2015 soil bearing and lateral pressure requirements. READ for the exact engineering design values.\n* **Block 6**: notes — General and structural notes detailing concrete specifications, clear cover minimums, and reinforcement rules. READ for material grades, cover depths, and corner bar requirements (24\"x24\").\n* **Block 9**: table — Required Inspections list. READ to confirm the three key inspection milestones (footings, walls, backfill/drainage).\n* **Blocks 5, 7, 8**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains a general schematic disclaimer (Block 5), title block data for project 3759 Valley View (Block 7), and engineer seals for MLAW Engineers (Block 8).\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776446612764,"pid":76090,"hostname":"Williams-MBP.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_016X5DZCC1oXYwEFhCirz3G9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The wall plan shows retaining walls with structural details. I need to check if these walls are near the overhead electric lines and if clearance dimensions are provided. Let me look at the overall site plan again (Sheet 9) to see where the walls are in relation to the overhead utilities shown on Sheet 4. The guide mentions a \"plan view\" showing wall locations.\n\nActually, I've already identified several not-verifiable items related to building and site feature clearances. Let me think about which other items are most critical and likely to have evidence:\n\n1. EL-1.2 - 15-foot radial clearance from distribution facilities (applies to buildings as customer facilities) - NOT-VERIFIABLE, no dimension lines\n2. EL-1.9 - Fences maintain 7.5-foot clearance - Need to check if fences are shown\n3. EL-1.10 - Fence posts 10 feet from poles - Need to check\n4. EL-1.14 - Retaining walls maintain 7.5-foot clearance - Likely NOT-VERIFIABLE without dimensions\n5. EL-1.17 - Retaining wall clearance sections - Likely NOT-VERIFIABLE\n6. EL-1.19 - Parking areas clearance - Likely NOT-VERIFIABLE\n7. EL-1.20 - Roads under transmission - Need to determine if transmission facilities exist\n8. EL-1.22 - Poles in driveway conflicts - Need to check plan\n9. EL-1.24 - Streetlights under lines - Need to check photometric plan\n10. EL-1.25 - Sidewalks clearing poles - Likely NOT-VERIFIABLE\n11. EL-1.26 - Trash areas under utilities - Need to check site plan\n12. EL-1.27 - Customer facilities under easement - Critical, need to verify\n\n\n13. EL-1.28 - Water meters clearance - Need to check utility plan\n14. EL-1.29 - Water lines clearance - Need to check\n15. EL-1.36 - Decorative lighting - Need to check\n16. EL-1.38 - Meter obstructions - Need to check\n17. EL-1.39 - Streetlight clearance - Need to check\n18. EL-1.40 - Down guys - Need to check survey\n19. EL-1.43 - Transformer clearance - Need to check utility plan\n20. EL-1.45 - Construction staging clearance - Likely N/A\n\nSeveral of these items require cross-referencing utility plans and site layouts to verify clearances. Water meter and line clearances, decorative lighting placement, and transformer locations all depend on what's shown in the utility coordination documents. The survey should indicate down guy locations, and the photometric plan would show streetlight positions relative to overhead lines.\n\nThe key distinction is between items that can't be verified due to missing documentation versus those that actually violate requirements. Since no dimension lines appear on the main plans showing clearances, and overhead conductor elevations aren't surveyed on building sections, I should mark these as not-verifiable rather than passing. The trees planted within 10 feet of utilities without documented species information represents a similar gap—it's a documentation issue rather than a confirmed code violation.\n\nFor the 48-hour notice requirement, I can see the pre-construction safety meeting note on Sheet 3 that references contacting Austin Energy 48 hours before construction near transmission lines. This does address the notification requirement, though it's specifically tied to transmission work rather than general overhead line work.\n\nThe code citation points to OSHA clearance standards that apply to both distribution and transmission lines during construction. However, the 48-hour notice documentation only appears under the Transmission Notes section—there's no equivalent notice requirement listed for distribution line work in the Electric Notes section, which covers tree pruning, easements, and contractor clearance responsibilities instead.\n\nThe underlying requirement comes from Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003, which mandates notification before work near power lines. While the transmission note satisfies this for high-voltage transmission work, I'm uncertain whether a similar notice is needed for distribution line construction. Since this is a construction safety matter that typically gets addressed during plan review rather than shown on the site plan itself, this might be better classified as not-verifiable rather than a failure.\n\nLooking at the output requirements, I should only flag items marked as \"fail\" or \"not-verifiable,\" excluding passes and n/a items. The transmission line notice is present on Sheet 3, so that portion passes. For distribution work, I can't verify whether the requirement applies or if notice is provided, making it not-verifiable.\n\nLet me focus on what clearly needs attention. Several items are missing critical clearance dimensions—EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.8, EL-1.18, EL-1.23, and EL-1.31 all lack the required measurements.\n\nAdditionally, surveyed elevations aren't documented for EL-1.7, tree species identification is absent for EL-1.37 near utilities, and clearance exhibits are missing for EL-1.17. These gaps prevent verification of compliance rather than proving violations.\n\nBefore identifying actual failures, I need to determine what overhead facilities exist on site and whether the vertical clearance standards—25-foot, 30-foot, or 40-foot—actually apply. Sheet 3 references transmission equipment and lines, suggesting they may be present or adjacent to the property.\n\nThe electric easements shown in facts.md and the overhead poles visible on Sheet 4 indicate utility infrastructure exists. Sheet 3's 16 transmission-related notes point toward transmission facilities being involved, though without voltage specifications on the plans, I can't definitively distinguish between transmission (≥60kV) and distribution (<60kV) systems. This classification uncertainty means items like EL-1.20 and EL-1.21 that hinge on facility type cannot be verified as compliant or non-compliant.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2667,"cache_read_input_tokens":70157,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2667},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97c9d1ef-3ef8-4a72-b129-cbdede465e25","uuid":"7831d46d-0970-4c90-b11b-f0e953ddb1f2"}
